index e106b7984..ae25dbedd 100644
--- a/CREDITS-AMD.md
+++ b/CREDITS-AMD.md
@@ -22,3 +22,7 @@ Daniel Han and Unsloth supply the shared DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash-1017 implementation
 ## Multi-GPU MoE prefill distribution
 
 Paul D. Martin, Ph.D., supplies the layer-keyed distribution with round-robin fallback and the routing-readback bypass, adapted from the [performance notebook patch](https://github.com/pauldmartinphd/llm-performance-engineering-notebook/blob/main/patches/prefill/README.md). See [port details and validation](docs/moe-prefill-offload.md). Both are enabled by default for eligible prefill operations; `GGML_MOE_PREFILL=0` disables them.
+
+## Experimental host-weight pipeline
+
+Roman Marchenko (drrros) supplies the host-weight pipeline implementation from [upstream PR 29963](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/pull/29963), revision `3ca7eed13274e406822fa1ba3e69a883560566bc`. The imported seven-file diff adds single-copy host weights and user inputs, pipeline graph reservation, layer-local offload, and balanced automatic fitting. The integration retains Paul Martin's existing non-pipeline routing/readback policy. This import is confined to `experimental/perf-29963`; it retains the source project's MIT license.
diff --git a/common/fit.cpp b/common/fit.cpp
index 7a0300829..708503c63 100644
--- a/common/fit.cpp
+++ b/common/fit.cpp
@@ -657,6 +657,82 @@ static void common_params_fit_impl(
         overflow_bufts.push_back(ggml_backend_cpu_buffer_type());
     }
 
+    // with pipeline parallelism, each device uploads the MoE weights in system memory of its own layers (keep the conditions in sync with llama_context)
+    // so give each device an equal slice of the layers and convert as many of its dense-only layers to full layers as fit
+    bool pipeline_parallel = nd > 1 && hp_nex > 0 && global_surplus_cpu_moe > 0 &&
+        mparams->split_mode == LLAMA_SPLIT_MODE_LAYER && cparams->op_offload && cparams->offload_kqv;
+    for (ggml_backend_dev_t dev : devs) {
+        ggml_backend_dev_props props;
+        ggml_backend_dev_get_props(dev, &props);
+        pipeline_parallel = pipeline_parallel &&
+            (ggml_backend_dev_type(dev) == GGML_BACKEND_DEVICE_TYPE_CPU || (props.caps.async && props.caps.events));
+    }
+
+    if (pipeline_parallel) {
+        LOG_TRC("%s: filling equal slices of dense-only layers for pipeline parallelism:\n", __func__);
+        std::vector<ngl_t> ngl_per_device_slice(nd);
+        std::vector<uint32_t> n_convert_low(nd, 0); // number of converted layers that fits
+        std::vector<uint32_t> n_convert_high(nd);   // number of converted layers that does not fit
+        uint32_t n_unassigned = hp_ngl + 1;
+        for (size_t id = 0; id < nd; id++) {
+            ngl_per_device_slice[id].n_layer = n_unassigned / (nd - id);
+            ngl_per_device_slice[id].n_part  = id < nd - 1 ? ngl_per_device_slice[id].n_layer : ngl_per_device_slice[id].n_layer - 1; // the output layer must be full
+            n_unassigned -= ngl_per_device_slice[id].n_layer;
+            n_convert_high[id] = ngl_per_device_slice[id].n_part + 1;
+        }
+
+        // bisect all devices at once, the memory use of a device depends a bit on the other devices so the result is measured again at the end
+        bool fits = true;
+        while (fits) {
+            bool done = true;
+            for (size_t id = 0; id < nd; id++) {
+                done = done && n_convert_high[id] - n_convert_low[id] <= 1;
+            }
+            std::vector<ngl_t> ngl_per_device_test = ngl_per_device_slice;
+            std::vector<uint32_t> n_convert(nd);
+            for (size_t id = 0; id < nd; id++) {
+                n_convert[id] = done ? n_convert_low[id] : (n_convert_low[id] + n_convert_high[id]) / 2;
+                ngl_per_device_test[id].n_part -= n_convert[id];
+            }
+            const std::vector<int64_t> mem_test = get_memory_for_layers(__func__, ngl_per_device_test, overflow_bufts);
+            for (size_t id = 0; id < nd; id++) {
+                if (mem_test[id] <= targets[id]) {
+                    n_convert_low[id] = n_convert[id];
+                } else if (n_convert[id] > n_convert_low[id]) {
+                    n_convert_high[id] = n_convert[id];
+                } else {
+                    fits = false;
+                }
+            }
+            if (done && fits) {
+                // try to fit part of one more layer on each device, like below
+                std::vector<int64_t> mem = mem_test;
+                for (const common_layer_fraction_t lf : {LAYER_FRACTION_GATE, LAYER_FRACTION_UP}) {
+                    std::vector<ngl_t> ngl_per_device_part = ngl_per_device_test;
+                    for (size_t id = 0; id < nd; id++) {
+                        if (ngl_per_device_part[id].n_part > 0 && ngl_per_device_part[id].overflow_type == LAYER_FRACTION_MOE) {
+                            ngl_per_device_part[id].overflow_type = lf;
+                        }
+                    }
+                    const std::vector<int64_t> mem_part = get_memory_for_layers(__func__, ngl_per_device_part, overflow_bufts);
+                    for (size_t id = 0; id < nd; id++) {
+                        if (ngl_per_device_part[id].overflow_type == lf && mem_part[id] <= targets[id]) {
+                            ngl_per_device_test[id].overflow_type = lf;
+                            mem[id] = mem_part[id];
+                        }
+                    }
+                }
+                for (size_t id = 0; id < nd; id++) {
+                    LOG_TRC("%s:   - %s: %2" PRIu32 " layers (%2" PRIu32 " overflowing), %6" PRId64 " MiB used, %6" PRId64 " MiB free\n",
+                        __func__, dev_names[id].c_str(), ngl_per_device_test[id].n_layer, ngl_per_device_test[id].n_part, mem[id]/MiB, (dmds_full[id].free - mem[id])/MiB);
+                }
+                set_ngl_tensor_split_tbo(ngl_per_device_test, overflow_bufts, *mparams);
+                return;
+            }
+        }
+        LOG_TRC("%s: equal slices of layers do not fit, falling back\n", __func__);
+    }
+
     std::vector<ngl_t> ngl_per_device(nd);
     std::vector<int64_t> mem = get_memory_for_layers(__func__, ngl_per_device, overflow_bufts);
 
diff --git a/docs/expert-execution.md b/docs/expert-execution.md
new file mode 100644
index 000000000..d7fa79a10
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/expert-execution.md
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+# Isolated candidate: host-weight pipeline parallelism
+
+Roman Marchenko's [PR 29963](https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/pull/29963), pinned to `3ca7eed13274e406822fa1ba3e69a883560566bc`, supplies the implementation, imported as a seven-file diff against its upstream merge base. It shares a single device copy of host weights across pipeline slots, uploads user inputs before the splits, preserves worst-case graph reservations, assigns host-weight operations to their layer's device, and balances layer slices during automatic fitting.
+
+This is inter-GPU, multi-ubatch prompt processing. It is not concurrent CPU/GPU expert decoding or Maya's within-layer streamed expert execution. Eligibility requires multiple devices, layer splitting, sufficient GPU-layer offload, asynchronous execution/events, and sufficiently distributed host weights. Short prompts offer little overlap; asymmetric GPU/PCIe systems can regress. The source discussion reports such regressions, so this remains experimental.
+
+The integration resolves the overlapping routing-readback logic by using the imported pipeline policy during pipeline execution and retaining Paul Martin's existing density policy otherwise. The existing scheduler routing patch remains present. The imported code replaces the cache-disabled-only pipeline restriction; GPU testing must cover cache both enabled and disabled, successive prompts, and prompt/decode transitions.
+
+Local validation: the ARM CPU library and common library compile, and all 33 allocator tests pass. These tests do not validate multiple HIP streams or establish a speedup. No AMD hardware benchmark has been run for this import.
+
+
+This branch starts at amd-main e3f526dd7 and contains only PR 29963 plus its integration conflict resolution. It excludes the provisional MXFP4 batching and upload-staging changes. Keep automatic fitting disabled for the first fixed-layout comparison; test fitting separately.
